<body>
    <h1>Landmarks: Performing Face Recognition</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>Alongside the core features of Vision Framework, we=E2=80=99ve g=
ot something called Landmarks, which lets us detect faces in images using m=
achine learning models trained to recognize facial patterns. When processin=
g an image, it looks for typical face features like eyes, nose, and mouth, =
and returns the coordinates that mark where the face is located.</p>
<p>There are two main types of face detection requests:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>VNDetectFaceRectanglesRequest</strong>: This one only finds the =
bounding boxes around the faces. It doesn=E2=80=99t give you any details ab=
out the face=E2=80=99s inner structure.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</strong>: This one not only detects=
 faces but also identifies facial landmarks like eyes, eyebrows, nose, mout=
h, and face contours. It lets you get a more detailed look at the expressio=
n and structure of the face.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>And that=E2=80=99s what we=E2=80=99re gonna talk about here: how to use =
<code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</code> to detect the main points on a fa=
ce, like the eyes, eyebrows, nose, mouth, and contours.</p>
<h2 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-UnderstandingVNDetectFaceLand=
marksRequest">Understanding VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</h2>
<p><strong>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</strong> is a class in the Vision F=
ramework that fits into the face detection pipeline by giving a detailed an=
alysis of the landmarks on a face.</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-WhatexactlyisVNDetectFaceLand=
marksRequestandhowdoesitfitintofacedetection?">What exactly is <code>VNDete=
ctFaceLandmarksRequest</code> and how does it fit into face detection?</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>
<p><code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</code> is a request that, once faces =
are found in an image (either by another request or as part of this one), p=
rocesses each face to identify its landmarks like the eyes, eyebrows, nose,=
 mouth, and face contours.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Pipeline Integration:</strong> Usually, the face detection flow =
starts by identifying the rough area of the face using <code>VNDetectFaceRe=
ctanglesRequest</code>. Then, you use <code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</c=
ode> to zoom in on the details and get precise info about the structure of =
the face. That allows for more advanced stuff, like analyzing expressions o=
r mapping the face for augmented reality.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Whatkindsoffaciallandmarkscan=
thisclassdetect?">What kinds of facial landmarks can this class detect?</h3=
>
<p><code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</code> can spot a bunch of different =
facial reference points, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Face contour</strong>: Shows the outer edges of the face.</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p><strong>Eyes</strong>: Detects the position and shape of the eyes (both =
left and right).</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Eyebrows</strong>: Identifies the shape and position of the eyeb=
rows and can sometimes even tell apart the upper and lower parts.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Nose</strong>: Maps out the bridge of the nose and its side cont=
ours.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Mouth</strong>: Detects the lip line and can include both upper =
and lower lip contours.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Other details</strong>: Depending on how it=E2=80=99s implemente=
d and how good the image is, it might also pick up on smaller features like=
 the corners of the eyes or specific areas of the face contour.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Whatarethemainpropertiesandme=
thodsofVNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest?">What are the main properties and meth=
ods of <code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</code>?</h3>
<p>Here are some of the most important ones:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>regionOfInterest:</strong> Lets you define a specific part of th=
e image to analyze, so you can focus only on areas that matter.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>inputFaceObservations:</strong> Lets you pass in faces that were=
 already detected (like from <code>VNDetectFaceRectanglesRequest</code>) to=
 make processing faster by focusing on pre-identified areas.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>completionHandler:</strong> This is a block of code that runs wh=
en the request finishes and gives you the results (the detected landmarks) =
so you can use them in your app.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Inherited Properties:</strong> Like with other Vision Framework =
requests, you=E2=80=99ve got options to tweak the accuracy, performance, an=
d other important stuff when processing the image.</p></li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Howarewegonnauseit?">How are =
we gonna use it?</h3>
<p>We're gonna use the Vision, AppKit and Foundation libraries to build an =
app that detects the main points of a face in an image, and in the end we'l=
l return the image with those points marked.</p>
<p>In the flow below, you can see how our app=E2=80=99s structure is gonna =
look:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapp=
er confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image=
 image-center" alt=3D"image.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"706f=
5eb69c741a409b3a71f08f9c098d5e7843ea5e9f5f1ec3061efefb60bbd3" data-image-sr=
c=3D"https://developeracademy.atlassian.net/wiki/download/attachments/12088=
77134/image.png?version=3D1&amp;modificationDate=3D1750692275707&amp;cacheV=
ersion=3D1&amp;api=3Dv2" data-height=3D"1045" data-width=3D"3824" data-unre=
solved-comment-count=3D"0" data-linked-resource-id=3D"1208877146" data-link=
ed-resource-version=3D"1" data-linked-resource-type=3D"attachment" data-lin=
ked-resource-default-alias=3D"image.png" data-base-url=3D"https://developer=
academy.atlassian.net/wiki" data-linked-resource-content-type=3D"image/png"=
 data-linked-resource-container-id=3D"1208877134" data-linked-resource-cont=
ainer-version=3D"1" data-media-id=3D"5c512b5e-1da4-492b-89af-42992bc1c230" =
data-media-type=3D"file" height=3D"127"></span>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>Just like we mentioned in the Vision Framework Introduction, we=E2=80=99=
ve got a base structure we follow when using the framework.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>We actually used a similar setup in the Rectangle Recognition example. T=
he examples are pretty close and so=E2=80=99s the implementation.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Development">Development</h1>
<p>We're gonna build a command-line app (Command Line Tool) on macOS that d=
oes the following steps:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Load the image</strong>: Reads an image from a file</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Process the image</strong>: Detects face landmarks using the Vis=
ion Framework</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Draw and export</strong>: Draws the detected points over the ima=
ge and saves the result as a new file</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s break down each step of the code.</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Firststep:Loadingtheimage">Fi=
rst step: Loading the image</h3>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func loa=
dImage(at path: String) -&gt; NSImage? {
    let fileURL =3D URL(fileURLWithPath: path)
    guard let image =3D NSImage(contentsOf: fileURL) else {
        print("Error: Couldn't load the image.")
        return nil
    }
    return image
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #E6FCFF;">
<p>We=E2=80=99re converting <code>NSImage</code> to <code>CGImage</code> so=
 we can use <code>VNImageRequestHandler</code>, which requires that format.=
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func nsI=
mageToCGImage(_ image: NSImage) -&gt; CGImage? {
    guard let imageData =3D image.tiffRepresentation,
          let bitmap =3D NSBitmapImageRep(data: imageData) else { return ni=
l }
    return bitmap.cgImage
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-DetectingLandmarks">Detecting=
 Landmarks</h3>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func det=
ectFaceLandmarks(in image: CGImage, completion: @escaping ([VNFaceObservati=
on]?) -&gt; Void) {
    let request =3D VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest { request, error in
        if let error =3D error {
            print("Landmark detection error: \\(error.localizedDescription)=
")
            completion(nil)
            return
        }
        let results =3D request.results as? [VNFaceObservation]
        completion(results)
    }
   =20
    let handler =3D VNImageRequestHandler(cgImage: image, options: [:])
    try? handler.perform([request])
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Here we=E2=80=99re connecting the <code>VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest</co=
de> to our <code>VNImageRequestHandler</code>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-What=E2=80=99sVNFaceObservati=
on?">What=E2=80=99s <code>VNFaceObservation</code> ?</h3>
<p>The result of this function gives us an object of type <code>VNFaceObser=
vation</code>, which is a class that represents the detected facial landmar=
ks. In other words, when the system spots a face in an image, it wraps that=
 info in a <code>VNFaceObservation</code>object. This object can also hold =
detailed data about facial landmarks when used with <code>VNDetectFaceLandm=
arksRequest</code>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-MainPropertiesofVNFaceObserva=
tion">Main Properties of <code>VNFaceObservation</code></h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>boundingBox:</strong></p>
<p>A <code>CGRect</code> structure that shows the face=E2=80=99s position a=
nd size in normalized coordinates (values between 0 and 1), relative to the=
 image=E2=80=99s size. This is essential to know where the face is.</p></li=
>
<li>
<p><strong>landmarks:</strong> A <code>VNFaceLandmarks2D</code> object that=
 contains the facial landmarks found, like the eyes, eyebrows, nose, mouth,=
 face contour and more. This is what gives us the detailed facial structure=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>roll:</strong></p>
<p>This value shows the tilt (rotation on the z-axis) of the face, usually =
in radians. It helps understand the face=E2=80=99s orientation in the image=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>yaw:</strong></p>
<p>This value shows the face=E2=80=99s side rotation (y-axis), also in radi=
ans. It tells us if the face is turned a bit to one side.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Behavior:</strong></p>
<p>Even though <code>VNFaceObservation</code> is mainly used to store detec=
tion results, it inherits methods and properties from the base class <code>=
VNObservation</code>. The class doesn=E2=80=99t really offer methods to man=
ipulate the data directly, it's more like a data container you use to draw =
landmarks on the image or run further analysis. In short, <code>VNFaceObser=
vation</code> gives you a structured way to access both the location and th=
e landmark details of faces, making it easier to build advanced facial reco=
gnition or analysis features.</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Drawingthepoints:">Drawing th=
e points:</h3>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func dra=
wFaceLandmarks(on image: CGImage, faceObservations: [VNFaceObservation], ou=
tputPath: String) {
    let size =3D CGSize(width: image.width, height: image.height)
   =20
    guard let colorSpace =3D image.colorSpace else {
        print("Error: Color space not found.")
        return
    }
   =20
    guard let context =3D CGContext(
        data: nil,
        width: image.width,
        height: image.height,
        bitsPerComponent: image.bitsPerComponent,
        bytesPerRow: 0,
        space: colorSpace,
        bitmapInfo: image.bitmapInfo.rawValue
    ) else {
        print("Error creating CGContext.")
        return
    }

    context.draw(image, in: CGRect(origin: .zero, size: size))
    context.setFillColor(NSColor.green.cgColor)
   =20
    // Helper function to draw the points of a landmark
    func drawPoints(for landmark: VNFaceLandmarkRegion2D?, in face: VNFaceO=
bservation) {
        guard let landmark =3D landmark else { return }
        for i in 0..&lt;landmark.pointCount {
            let normalizedPoint =3D landmark.normalizedPoints[i]
            // Converts the normalized point (relative to the face's boundi=
ngBox) to absolute image coordinates.
            let faceBoundingBox =3D face.boundingBox
            let absoluteX =3D faceBoundingBox.origin.x * CGFloat(image.widt=
h) + normalizedPoint.x * faceBoundingBox.size.width * CGFloat(image.width)
            let absoluteY =3D faceBoundingBox.origin.y * CGFloat(image.heig=
ht) + normalizedPoint.y * faceBoundingBox.size.height * CGFloat(image.heigh=
t)
           =20
            // Draws a small circle at the detected point
            let circleRect =3D CGRect(x: absoluteX - 2, y: absoluteY - 2, w=
idth: 4, height: 4)
            context.fillEllipse(in: circleRect)
        }
    }
   =20
    // Loops through each detected face and draws its landmarks
    for face in faceObservations {
        guard let landmarks =3D face.landmarks else { continue }
       =20
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.leftEye, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.rightEye, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.leftEyebrow, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.rightEyebrow, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.nose, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.noseCrest, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.medianLine, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.outerLips, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.innerLips, in: face)
        drawPoints(for: landmarks.faceContour, in: face)
    }
   =20
    if let newCGImage =3D context.makeImage() {
        let outputNSImage =3D NSImage(cgImage: newCGImage, size: size)
        if let tiffData =3D outputNSImage.tiffRepresentation,
           let bitmap =3D NSBitmapImageRep(data: tiffData),
           let pngData =3D bitmap.representation(using: .png, properties: [=
:]) {
            do {
                try pngData.write(to: URL(fileURLWithPath: outputPath))
                print("Image saved successfully at \\(outputPath)")
            } catch {
                print("Error saving image: \\(error.localizedDescription)")
            }
        }
    } else {
        print("Error creating image with landmarks.")
    }
}</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The <code>drawFaceLandmarks</code> function is a utility that reproduces=
 the result we got and draws the points on the image. Here's what we use:</=
p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><code>CGContext</code>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>This is the graphics context where all the drawing happens. You can thin=
k of it like a blank canvas where you draw shapes, images, and text.</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p>In the function, we create a <code>CGContext</code> with the same proper=
ties as the original image. If that fails, we show an error.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><code>VNImageRectForNormalizedRect</code>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>This function converts normalized rectangles (values between 0 and 1) in=
to actual pixel coordinates.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Since Vision Framework gives us normalized values, we need this conversi=
on to draw things properly on the image.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<div class=3D"panel" style=3D"background-color: #DEEBFF;border-width: 1px;"=
>
<div class=3D"panelContent" style=3D"background-color: #DEEBFF;">
<p>We also used a structure very similar to the one in the rectangle detect=
ion code.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p>And to use all the functions we built in the project, here=E2=80=99s how=
 it goes:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">// Main =
flow: Load image, detect landmarks and draw points
if let nsImage =3D loadImage(at: inputImagePath), let cgImage =3D nsImageTo=
CGImage(nsImage) {
    detectFaceLandmarks(in: cgImage) { faceObservations in
        guard let faceObservations =3D faceObservations, !faceObservations.=
isEmpty else {
            print("No face detected.")
            return
        }
        drawFaceLandmarks(on: cgImage, faceObservations: faceObservations, =
outputPath: outputImagePath)
    }
} else {
    print("Error loading image.")
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>And just like that, we generate a new image from the updated context. Th=
en we convert that image to <code>NSImage</code> and finally into PNG data.=
</p>
<h3 id=3D"Landmarks:PerformingFaceRecognition-Results">Results</h3><span cl=
ass=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-emb=
